## Code Quality

### TypeScript

- Always use strict type checking
- Define interfaces for test data structures
- Use type guards for response validation

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Type-safe
interface TestUser {
  id: string;
  email: string;
  phone: string;
}

function isValidUser(user: any): user is TestUser {
  return user && typeof user.id === 'string' && typeof user.email === 'string';
}

// ❌ Bad: Untyped
function createUser(data: any) {
  return data;
}
```

### Error Handling

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Specific error handling
try {
  await dangerousOperation();
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof GraphQLError) {
    console.error('GraphQL Error:', error.message);
  } else if (error instanceof NetworkError) {
    console.error('Network Error:', error.message);
  } else {
    throw error; // Re-throw unexpected errors
  }
}

// ❌ Bad: Silent failures
try {
  await dangerousOperation();
} catch (error) {
  // nothing
}
```

---

## Test Organization

### Feature-Based Structure

Organize tests by user-facing features, not by technical layers:

```
✅ Good:
tests/
├── user-registration/
├── device-activation/
├── subscription-purchase/
└── pet-management/

❌ Bad:
tests/
├── graphql/
├── database/
├── api/
└── integration/
```

### Test Suites

Group related tests using `describe` blocks:

```typescript
describe('Subscription Management', () => {
  describe('Purchase Flow', () => {
    it('should purchase monthly plan', async () => {});
    it('should purchase yearly plan', async () => {});
  });

  describe('Cancellation Flow', () => {
    it('should cancel auto-renewal', async () => {});
    it('should refund unused portion', async () => {});
  });
});
```

---

## Performance Considerations

- **Parallel execution**: Use `test.concurrent` for independent tests
- **Setup/teardown**: Use `beforeAll`/`afterAll` for expensive operations
- **Mock external services**: When appropriate, mock Chargebee/Twilio/SendGrid
- **Database connections**: Reuse connections, don't create new ones per test

---

## CI/CD Integration

Tests are run automatically on:
- Pull request creation
- Merge to main branch
- Scheduled nightly runs

**Environment-specific test runs:**
- `npm test` - runs all tests
- `npm test:unit` - unit tests only
- `npm test:integration` - integration tests only
- `npm test:e2e` - end-to-end tests only

